{"vid":"SC_opiKLohg","source":"SC_opiKLohg.en-GB.json3","lang":"en","cues":[{"t":8,"txt":"Hello, this is 6 Minute English from BBC Learning English. I'm Phil."},{"t":12.9,"txt":"And I'm Pippa."},{"t":14.2,"txt":"Here at 6 Minute English, we love discussing new tech."},{"t":18.2,"txt":"One of the most controversial technologies of recent years"},{"t":21.7,"txt":"is driverless cars, also known as self-driving cars,"},{"t":26.3,"txt":"autonomous cars or robotaxis."},{"t":29.3,"txt":"Many people say they wouldn't feel safe in a car without a human driver,"},{"t":34.2,"txt":"but there are concerns from other road users too –"},{"t":37.4,"txt":"pedestrians, runners and cyclists."},{"t":40.2,"txt":"How will driverless cars interact with them?"},{"t":43.1,"txt":"It's a problem Professor Stephen Brewster,"},{"t":45.6,"txt":"from Glasgow University, has been working on."},{"t":48.5,"txt":"He explained more to BBC World Service programme Tech Life."},{"t":52.7,"txt":"If you're outside a vehicle right now,"},{"t":54,"txt":"you can see the driver inside the vehicle,"},{"t":55.6,"txt":"you can wave to them, they can see you, you can make eye contact."},{"t":59.1,"txt":"But when you come to an autonomous vehicle,"},{"t":61,"txt":"there's no person in the driving seat,"},{"t":63,"txt":"so how do you negotiate whether they're going to go,"},{"t":66.9,"txt":"whether they're going to let you go?"},{"t":68.6,"txt":"On my walk to work, I need to cross the road."},{"t":71.4,"txt":"Usually a driver makes eye contact with me."},{"t":74.2,"txt":"They look directly into my eyes and then wave me across."},{"t":78,"txt":"'To wave' means to raise your hand and move it from side to side."},{"t":82.5,"txt":"It's a gesture for saying hello,"},{"t":84.6,"txt":"or a way of telling a person to do something."},{"t":87.3,"txt":"Luckily, Phil, you usually arrive safely at work"},{"t":90.9,"txt":"and as yet there are no driverless cars in London."},{"t":94.6,"txt":"But, Phil, how would you feel about crossing that road"},{"t":98.2,"txt":"in front of a robotaxi?"},{"t":100.2,"txt":"Well, maybe a little bit nervous."},{"t":102,"txt":"I might wait for it to go and go behind it or something."},{"t":105.2,"txt":"Mmm... yeah, I get that."},{"t":106.7,"txt":"I think maybe as we get more used to these technologies"},{"t":110.4,"txt":"we'll get more used to how to deal with them and when to cross."},{"t":114.4,"txt":"But in this episode we investigate"},{"t":117.3,"txt":"the tech helping driverless cars communicate on the roads."},{"t":120.8,"txt":"We'll learn some useful new words and phrases, and as usual you'll find"},{"t":125.4,"txt":"all the vocabulary on our website, bbclearningenglish.com."},{"t":129.9,"txt":"But first, I have a question for you, Pippa."},{"t":133.6,"txt":"As you mentioned, we don't have self-driving cars here in London yet."},{"t":137.7,"txt":"So, which was the first city to have fully driverless robotaxis"},{"t":143.1,"txt":"on the streets?"},{"t":144.2,"txt":"Was it: a) Philadelphia, b) Phoenix, or c) San Francisco?"},{"t":149.7,"txt":"I'm going to say b) Phoenix because I think it's quite a car-friendly city."},{"t":156.4,"txt":"OK. Well, we will find out the answer, but at the end of the programme."},{"t":160.8,"txt":"Because accidents are often caused by not seeing the oncoming danger,"},{"t":165.5,"txt":"Professor Brewster wants to make driverless cars more visible."},{"t":169.6,"txt":"He discussed his work with BBC Tech Life's presenter Chris Vallance."},{"t":175,"txt":"What kind of signals worked in terms of alerting pedestrians and runners"},{"t":180.5,"txt":"and cyclists to the intentions of the vehicle?"},{"t":183.8,"txt":"We tested two things."},{"t":184.7,"txt":"We tested animations and fixed, coloured light rings."},{"t":189,"txt":"The animations – they work well for pedestrians,"},{"t":191.1,"txt":"but when it came to runners and cyclists,"},{"t":193.6,"txt":"they have much less time to make their decisions,"},{"t":195.5,"txt":"so if you do a quick glance at a vehicle,"},{"t":197.6,"txt":"you don't really see the animation."},{"t":199.5,"txt":"It's important to alert people to driverless cars."},{"t":203.5,"txt":"To alert somebody means to warn them about a potential danger."},{"t":207.8,"txt":"Some cars do this with animations – displays of moving images."},{"t":211.9,"txt":"For example, a green man walking to signal it's safe to cross."},{"t":216.4,"txt":"Animations alert pedestrians but are not suitable for runners and cyclists,"},{"t":221.4,"txt":"who are travelling too fast to react."},{"t":223.5,"txt":"They only have time for a glance."},{"t":226.2,"txt":"'A glance at something' means a quick look."},{"t":229.2,"txt":"So, instead Stephen's team used coloured light rings, similar to traffic lights,"},{"t":234.4,"txt":"to warn cyclists and runners."},{"t":236.8,"txt":"Culture is another problem."},{"t":239,"txt":"The meaning of body language can change."},{"t":241.4,"txt":"For example, hand waving can mean 'please proceed' in one culture,"},{"t":246.2,"txt":"but 'thank you' in another."},{"t":248.2,"txt":"Ammar Al-Taie is a researcher at the Advanced Institute of Science"},{"t":252,"txt":"and Technology in South Korea."},{"t":254,"txt":"Here, Ammar tells BBC World Service's Tech Life"},{"t":258.2,"txt":"why Korean culture is especially suited to self-driving cars."},{"t":262.8,"txt":"In South Korea, for example, jaywalking is illegal,"},{"t":265.6,"txt":"whereas in the UK, you know, we tend to jaywalk a lot."},{"t":268.8,"txt":"'Jaywalking' is an American-English word."},{"t":271.5,"txt":"It means crossing the road at a place where crossing is not allowed or"},{"t":275.6,"txt":"without taking care."},{"t":277,"txt":"Unlike Britain, jaywalking is rare in South Korea,"},{"t":280.6,"txt":"making the country safer for autonomous cars."},{"t":283.7,"txt":"We've heard a lot about making driverless cars safer,"},{"t":287.5,"txt":"and with plans to introduce them to London later this year,"},{"t":291.2,"txt":"we'll soon see how safe they really are."},{"t":294.3,"txt":"Now, isn't it time to reveal the answer to your question, Phil?"},{"t":298.1,"txt":"I asked you which city was the first to have self-driving cars on the road,"},{"t":302.5,"txt":"and you said it was Phoenix..."},{"t":304.5,"txt":"and that is the correct answer."},{"t":307.2,"txt":"Phoenix, Arizona, was the first city in the world to have"},{"t":311.1,"txt":"self-driving cars operating on public roads without a human safety driver,"},{"t":315.2,"txt":"and that was in 2017."},{"t":317.2,"txt":"Let's recap the vocabulary we've learned, starting with 'eye contact' –"},{"t":321.8,"txt":"looking directly into someone's eyes."},{"t":324.9,"txt":"'To wave at someone' is a way of saying hi,"},{"t":327.7,"txt":"by raising your hand and waving it from side to side."},{"t":330.8,"txt":"If you alert someone, you warn them of potential danger."},{"t":334.3,"txt":"An animation is a display made up of moving images."},{"t":338.4,"txt":"A glance is a quick, brief look at something."},{"t":341.4,"txt":"And finally, the American-English term 'jaywalking' means crossing the road"},{"t":346.4,"txt":"where you're not supposed to."},{"t":348,"txt":"Once again, our six minutes are up."},{"t":350.2,"txt":"If you'd like to hear more programmes about technology,"},{"t":352.9,"txt":"you'll find plenty on our tech topic page, all on our website."},{"t":357.2,"txt":"And there you'll also find a quiz and worksheet for this episode."},{"t":361.1,"txt":"Visit bbclearningenglish.com."},{"t":363.8,"txt":"We'll see you again soon, but for now it's goodbye."},{"t":366.7,"txt":"Goodbye!"}],"words":[{"t":7.96,"w":"Hello,"},{"t":7.96,"w":"this"},{"t":7.96,"w":"is"},{"t":7.96,"w":"6"},{"t":7.96,"w":"Minute"},{"t":7.96,"w":"English"},{"t":7.96,"w":"from"},{"t":7.96,"w":"BBC"},{"t":7.96,"w":"Learning"},{"t":7.96,"w":"English."},{"t":7.96,"w":"I'm"},{"t":7.96,"w":"Phil."},{"t":12.88,"w":"And"},{"t":12.88,"w":"I'm"},{"t":12.88,"w":"Pippa.